description:
Locations: src/filenode.rs:38-50 (handle_filenode_get), src/filenode.rs:904-918 (handle_filenode_query).

The repeated pattern:

    let (account_id, args_map) = extract_account_id(args)?;
    // ... use account_id for backend.get_ancestors / depth handling ...

    // Reconstitute the args object for the generic handler. The generic helper
    // re-parses accountId itself, which is fine — the duplicate parse is cheap.
    let mut reconstituted = args_map;
    reconstituted.insert(
        "accountId".to_owned(),
        Value::String(account_id.as_ref().to_owned()),
    );
    let (mut response, tail) = jmap_server::handlers::handle_get::<FileNode, B>(
        backend,
        caller,
        Value::Object(reconstituted),
    )
    .await?;

What is wrong with this:

1. extract_account_id (defined in jmap-server/src/helpers.rs:125) consumes the args Value, returning (Id, Map) where accountId has been REMOVED from the Map. The caller needs to use account_id locally for fetchParents / depth handling, AND wants to pass the original args through to handle_get. The reconstitute is purely to undo extract_account_id's destructive parse.

2. The String allocation in 'Value::String(account_id.as_ref().to_owned())' allocates the accountId again every call, just to re-stuff it into a Map where it was already living.

3. The comment 'the duplicate parse is cheap' is a confession that the abstraction is wrong, not a justification.

What idiomatic Rust would do — a few options, in increasing order of API change:

(a) Add a sibling helper to jmap-server::helpers: peek_account_id(&args) -> Result<Id, JmapError> that does not consume args. Use it in handlers that need account_id BEFORE delegating to handle_get. Existing extract_account_id is the destructive variant.

(b) Change extract_account_id to NOT strip accountId — it is the only caller that wants stripping, and stripping costs every other caller a reconstitute.

(c) Refactor handle_get to accept (Id, Map) instead of Value, sidestepping the question.

The supplement-level point: the strip-then-restore round-trip is the symptom of an abstraction mismatch. Two handlers in this crate go through the dance; no other extension-server sibling does. The fix is one upstream helper change, not eight in-handler workarounds across the workspace.

Canonical comparison: jmap-mail-server does NOT use the generic jmap_server::handlers::handle_get<...> at all — it writes per-method handlers inline. So mail-server never hits this round-trip. filenode is the only consumer of the generic handler, and it is the only crate that pays the reconstitute cost.

Workspace propagation: if option (b) is taken, every other call site of extract_account_id (13+ in mail-server alone) needs verification. The 'cheap fix' is option (a) — pure addition, no migration.

Filed under idiomatic-rust,clippy (priority 2) since this is an abstraction-smell finding, not a strict idiom violation. The code works; the shape is non-idiomatic.
